  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nThrough Thursday: Continued hot and humid along with dry weather. \nLatest satellite imagery/RAP analysis showed the circulation around \na large and anomalously stout mid level high centered over the \nTennessee valley. This large high will shift slightly west through \nThursday. Latest surface analysis had a >1020mb centered over the \nsouthern Appalachians that will continue to ridge back across our \nCWA through Thursday. Although near record high temperatures are \npossible at Greenville and Greenwood Thursday, drier air from the \neast will lower humidity and therefore help mitigate heat stress \nconcerns. Peak heat index values Thursday will approach 105F over \nthe western half of the CWA with lower heat index values to the \neast. /22/\n\nHigh pressure will remain over the Southeast CONUS heading into the \nweekend. The high pressure will gradually move westward and an upper-\nlvl low will increase moisture/humidity across the area late Sunday \ninto next week. Diurnal rain chances will return Monday and last \nthrough the mid-week. Although 15-30% rain chances will be possible \nacross the area, heat stress concerns are likely as highs remain \nabove-average to near record high temps over the forecast period. \n/SW/",
